                "text": "**Analyzing the Pattern**\n\nI've examined the color transformation example and deciphered the mapping. It seems each input color is replaced by another, following a specific cyclical pattern: 1 becomes 3, 2 becomes 1, and 3 becomes 2. I need to formulate a rule that describes this color-swapping.\n\n\n**Discovering the Cycle**\n\nI've been analyzing a second example to confirm the pattern. It appears the color cycle isn't tied to the specific objects, rows, or positions, but rather to a cyclical shift based on the *order* of the objects. It's a cyclic shift of color values in the order in which they appear in the input! The last color in the input sequence becomes the first, and so on.\n\n\n**Investigating the Order**\n\nI'm now diving into a third example, and I think I see a deeper pattern, perhaps tied to color values themselves, not just the cycle. This example suggests a more complex cyclical relationship between the colors: 2 becomes 7, then 7 becomes 3, 1 becomes 2, and finally, 3 becomes 1. I'm exploring different sorting methods to verify my observations.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Mapping**\n\nI've just focused on specific color transitions in the third example, tracing the \"where\" of each color in the input to its replacement in the output. The mapping: 1 becomes 2, 2 becomes 7, 7 becomes 3, and 3 becomes 1. This cycle holds regardless of object size or position, confirming a direct color-to-color replacement pattern, finally!\n\n\n**Unveiling the Spatial Dimension**\n\nI've been re-evaluating the color transformation, this time focusing on spatial positions. I started sorting colors by column to see if there was a connection between the color's position and its transformation. It turns out that I was right! The color that becomes the last color in the column order becomes the first, and then the next color in the column order gets the color from the previous one, and so on. It works perfectly! I've confirmed that this also applies to prior examples.\n\n\n",
